sentences.l:
Out they stripped me, indeed, of both cloak and tunic, clothes,
and about me another evil rag they threw, and a tunic,
torn, which you yourself also see with your eyes;

but since to them it did not appear better
to search farther, they, indeed, back again went
onto the hollow ship;

but me hid the gods themselves
easily, and me to a steading brought near, leading me,
of a man of understanding;

But they, indeed, each thing sitting beside ask out,
both those who grieve for the lord long gone,
and those who rejoice, his livelihood unpunished devouring;

If, indeed, your lord should come home to this house,
having clothed me in cloak and tunic, clothes, send me
to go to Dulichium, where it has become dear to me in my heart;

“Stranger, so, indeed, for me good fame and excellence
would be among men, both at once and afterwards,
for me who, when I led you to the hut and gave the things due to guests,
afterward should kill you and take your dear life away;

These, indeed, then they penned in to sleep in their accustomed haunts,
and a boundless clamour arose of the swine lodging in the yard;
and he, to his own companions, called out, the heavenly swineherd:
